														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1840832">
									

<p>Spider Robinson brought up a semi-related point in his short story &quot;Melancholy Elephants&quot;. To oversimplify, the human ear is only capable of distinguishing a finite number of sounds and those sounds can only be arranged in a finite number of ways. So there is a strict limit on how many songs are possible. To be sure, the set of all possible tunes is a HUGE number; but it's not infinite. That's not even considering that a great many of those arrangements would be a cacophony, not something anyone would interpret as music.</p>
<p>Maybe what Erich has found is the formula that maps the position patterns of &quot;true music&quot; (the arrangements that a human brain considers non-cacophony) among that total set.</p>

							</div>

								
					
